\section{Analytical Widening of the Stability Certificate}
\label{sec:stability-widening}

The preceding results deliberately state the weakest source-faithful hypotheses
needed for the implemented filter.  This section develops stronger sufficient
conditions that replace several trajectory-by-trajectory certificate
obligations by analytical conditions on physical geometry, update gaps, and
bounded parameter envelopes.  These results do not change the estimator or its
tuning law.  They widen the class of source-realizable trajectories for which
the existing Live, startup, and hybrid stability theorems can be invoked.

\subsection{Phase A: analytical vector observability and adaptation-independent Live UES}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-a}

The finite-window conditions in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-pe} and
\eqref{eq:iss-eta6-pe} remain the most general route because they allow arbitrary
maneuvers and asynchronous accepted measurements.  For the marine-motion
regime, however, a more explicit sufficient condition can be obtained from two
well-separated specific-force--magnetic vector packets.  The construction below
is conservative by design: it trades general PE for quantities that can be
bounded from sensor geometry and source timing.

For a nonzero body vector $\vct u$, let
$\mat J(\vct u):=[\vct u]_\times^\top[\vct u]_\times$.
Suppose an accepted accelerometer--magnetometer packet, after transporting the
two rows to a common packet time with the known nominal attitude transition,
has vectors $\vct f$ and $\vct m$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 \|\vct f\|\ge f_-,\qquad
 \|\vct m\|\ge m_-,\qquad
 \frac{\|\vct f\times\vct m\|}{\|\vct f\|\,\|\vct m\|}
 \ge s_{fm}>0.
 \label{eq:widen-vector-packet}
\end{equation}
Let the largest accepted three-axis covariance eigenvalues in the packet be
$r_{a,+}$ and $r_{m,+}$, and define
\begin{equation}
 a_f:=\frac{f_-^2}{r_{a,+}},\qquad
 a_m:=\frac{m_-^2}{r_{m,+}},\qquad
 \mu_\theta:=\min\{a_f,a_m\}
 \left(1-\sqrt{1-s_{fm}^2}\right)>0.
 \label{eq:widen-vector-mu}
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Explicit vector-pair attitude coercivity]
\label{lem:widen-vector-coercivity}
Every packet satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-vector-packet} contributes attitude
information bounded below by
\begin{equation}
 \mat J(\vct f)/r_{a,+}+\mat J(\vct m)/r_{m,+}
 \succeq \mu_\theta\mat I_3.
 \label{eq:widen-vector-coercivity}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $\vct u=\vct f/\|\vct f\|$ and
$\vct v=\vct m/\|\vct m\|$.  Since
$[\vct u]_\times^\top[\vct u]_\times=\mat I-\vct u\vct u^\top$,
the packet matrix is bounded below by
\begin{equation}
 \min\{a_f,a_m\}
 \left(2\mat I-\vct u\vct u^\top-\vct v\vct v^\top\right).
\end{equation}
The eigenvalues of the matrix in parentheses are
$2$, $1+|\vct u^\top\vct v|$, and
$1-|\vct u^\top\vct v|$.  Equation~\eqref{eq:widen-vector-packet} gives
$|\vct u^\top\vct v|\le\sqrt{1-s_{fm}^2}$, which proves the claim.
\end{proof}

To expose gyro bias, consider two such packets separated by
$\Delta_g\in[\Delta_{g,-},\Delta_{g,+}]$.  In transported local coordinates the
homogeneous attitude/gyro-bias error over the separation has the source-consistent
left-multiplicative form
\begin{equation}
 \delta\vct\theta_1
 =\mat\Phi_\theta\delta\vct\theta_0
  +\mat\Gamma_g\delta\vct b_g,
 \qquad
 \mat\Gamma_g:=\int_0^{\Delta_g}\mat\Phi_\theta(\Delta_g,s)\,ds,
 \label{eq:widen-bg-transition}
\end{equation}
where $\mat\Phi_\theta$ is orthogonal for the skew-symmetric nominal rotation
part.  If the nominal body-rate magnitude is bounded by $\bar\omega$ on the
packet interval, then
\begin{equation}
 \|\mat\Gamma_g-\Delta_g\mat I\|
 \le \frac12\bar\omega\Delta_g^2.
 \label{eq:widen-gamma-perturbation}
\end{equation}
Consequently, whenever $\bar\omega\Delta_{g,+}<2$,
\begin{equation}
 \sigma_{\min}(\mat\Gamma_g)
 \ge g_-:=\Delta_{g,-}
 \left(1-\frac12\bar\omega\Delta_{g,+}\right)>0.
 \label{eq:widen-gamma-lower}
\end{equation}

Because attitude error and gyro-bias error have different physical units, state
the explicit singular-value bound in a fixed dimensionless proof coordinate.
Choose any declared time scale $T_{bg}>0$ and let
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde{\vct b}_g:=T_{bg}\delta\vct b_g,
 \qquad
 \widetilde\mat\Gamma_g:=\mat\Gamma_g/T_{bg},
 \qquad
 \gamma_-:=g_-/T_{bg}>0.
 \label{eq:widen-bg-scaling}
\end{equation}
This is only a constant change of norm; UCO and UES are invariant under it, and
the corresponding constants in the original physical coordinates follow by
fixed norm equivalence.

\begin{lemma}[Two-packet attitude/gyro-bias observability]
\label{lem:widen-two-packet-bg}
If every proof window contains two accepted packets satisfying
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-vector-packet}--\eqref{eq:widen-bg-scaling}, then the
held-bias attitude/gyro-bias Gramian in the dimensionless coordinate
$(\delta\theta,\widetilde b_g)$ has the explicit admissible lower bound
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \alpha_6^{\rm an}:=
 \frac{\mu_\theta}{1+2/\gamma_-^2}>0.}
 \label{eq:widen-alpha6}
\end{equation}
A finite upper bound $\beta_6^{\rm an}$ follows from the compact vector,
rate, covariance, window, and fixed scaling bounds.  By norm equivalence this
implies Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta6-pe} in any other fixed state scaling.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
After whitening each packet by its information square root,
Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-vector-coercivity} allows the two packet outputs to be
bounded below by $\sqrt{\mu_\theta}$ times the dimensionless triangular
observation map
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde\mat T_g=
 \begin{bmatrix}
  \mat I&0\\
  \mat\Phi_\theta&\widetilde\mat\Gamma_g
 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Given packet outputs $(\vct y_0,\vct y_1)$,
$\delta\vct\theta_0=\vct y_0$ and
$\widetilde\vct b_g=\widetilde\mat\Gamma_g^{-1}
(\vct y_1-\mat\Phi_\theta\vct y_0)$.  Orthogonality of
$\mat\Phi_\theta$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-bg-scaling}, and
$(a+b)^2\le2(a^2+b^2)$ give
$\|\widetilde\mat T_g^{-1}\|^2\le1+2/\gamma_-^2$.
Hence
$\sigma_{\min}^2(\widetilde\mat T_g)\ge
(1+2/\gamma_-^2)^{-1}$, yielding Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-alpha6}.
Boundedness of the same finite stacked operator gives the upper Gramian bound.
\end{proof}

The bound is intentionally a sufficient marine-motion condition, not a
replacement for the general PE route.  Aggressive maneuvers for which
$\bar\omega\Delta_{g,+}\ge2$ may still satisfy Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta6-pe}; they
simply use the original Gramian condition rather than
Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-two-packet-bg}.  For the active 21-state mode, the deployed
finite residual-accelerometer-bias correlation time then invokes route (ii) of
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues}.  Thus full nine-state PE is not required for this
analytical route.  The direct nine-state condition Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-pe}
remains available when the accelerometer-bias model is intentionally taken to
its random-walk limit.

\subsubsection{Envelope switching rather than tuner-specific slew}

Define the adaptation-envelope family $\Pi^{\rm env}$ as all predictable Live
parameter and enable-state sequences satisfying
\begin{align}
 0<h_-&\le h_k\le h_+,&
 0<\tau_-&\le\tau_k\le\tau_+<\infty,\\
 0<r_-&\le r_{S,k}\le r_+<\infty,&
 0<\sigma_{aw,-}^{\rm proc}&\le\sigma_{aw,k}^{\rm proc}
 \le\sigma_{aw,+}^{\rm proc}<\infty,
 \label{eq:widen-envelope-bounds}
\end{align}
with accepted measurement covariances in fixed positive compact intervals and
usable $S$ firings satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds}.  No EMA recurrence,
parameter slew bound, wave-period scaling law, or particular power law is part
of $\Pi^{\rm env}$.

\begin{lemma}[Translational information is uniform on the envelope]
\label{lem:widen-envelope-translation}
Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco} and the translational process-excitation argument
remain valid for every sequence in $\Pi^{\rm env}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The determinant proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco} uses only the bounds on
$\tau(t)$, the proof-window length, and the lower/upper gaps between selected
$S$ firings.  It does not use the EMA recurrence or a bound on
$|\tau_{k+1}-\tau_k|$.  Likewise, the one-axis controllability determinant is
$-1$ for every finite $\lambda=1/\tau$.  The exact finite-interval process
Gramian is continuous on the compact set
$[h_-,h_+]\times[1/\tau_+,1/\tau_-]$ and is positive definite pointwise;
therefore its minimum eigenvalue has a positive uniform lower bound.  The
positive process-amplitude floor in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-envelope-bounds}
completes the uniform covariance bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Adaptation-independent full-heading Live UES]
\label{thm:widen-envelope-ues}
Assume the compact physical geometry and covariance bounds of
Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-two-packet-bg}, the translational conditions of
Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-envelope-translation}, no hard attitude rewrite inside a
normal-Live interval, and a finite bounded residual-accelerometer-bias
correlation time after bias release.  Then the held 18-state homogeneous error
transition and the active 21-state homogeneous error transition are UES
uniformly over every sequence $\pi\in\Pi^{\rm env}$.  In particular, linear
Live stability does not depend on the adaptation-law exponents, EMA horizons,
or the rate at which admissible values of $\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$, and $R_S$
move inside the certified envelope.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-two-packet-bg} supplies the held/reduced information bound
with the explicit constant in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-alpha6};
Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-envelope-translation} supplies uniform translational
observability and process excitation.  The remaining active process channels
and accepted measurement covariances have the same positive compact bounds as
in Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues}.  Therefore the UCO/UCC hypotheses of the
standard discrete LTV Kalman/Riccati stability result hold uniformly over
$\Pi^{\rm env}$.  Held accelerometer bias is a bounded input.  After release,
finite $\tau_b$ gives exponential stability of any residual unobserved
accelerometer-bias direction, hence uniform detectability.  The conclusion
follows exactly as in Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues}, now without invoking the
source EMA recurrence.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Meaning of Phase A]
\label{rem:widen-phase-a-meaning}
The deployed tuner remains source-reachable and is therefore a subset of
$\Pi^{\rm env}$.  Phase A does not claim that arbitrary unbounded tuning is
safe.  It shows instead that, inside declared compact parameter and update-gap
bounds, the adaptation algorithm is a performance mechanism rather than a
stability-critical feedback law.  The only new physical price paid for the
explicit observability route is the conservative two-packet vector geometry
and bounded-turn condition; trajectories outside that sufficient condition may
still use the original finite-window PE theorem.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Phase B: finite-window contraction and constructive Lyapunov margins}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-b}

The converse Lyapunov construction of Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-converse-P} is exact but
its constants are stated existentially.  Uniformity over the compact envelope
allows those constants to be reduced to a finite-window contraction bound.
Let $\widetilde\Psi(k,j)$ denote the homogeneous dimensionless active-error
transition in either source mode and define
\begin{equation}
 \bar A:=\sup_{k,\pi\in\Pi^{\rm env}}
 \|\widetilde\mat A_k^{\rm cl}(\pi)\|<\infty.
 \label{eq:widen-Abar}
\end{equation}
For a positive integer $N$, define the worst-case $N$-step gain
\begin{equation}
 \kappa_N:=
 \sup_{k,\pi\in\Pi^{\rm env}}
 \|\widetilde\Psi(k+N,k;\pi)\|.
 \label{eq:widen-kappaN}
\end{equation}
The supremum is over a finite product of matrices generated by the compact
physical and scheduling envelope.  Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-envelope-ues}
therefore implies that some finite $N$ has $\kappa_N<1$; conversely, any
verified pair $(N,\kappa_N)$ with $\kappa_N<1$ is already a direct uniform UES
certificate.

\begin{lemma}[Constructive UES constants from an $N$-step contraction]
\label{lem:widen-constructive-ues}
Suppose Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-kappaN} satisfies $0<\kappa_N<1$.  Define
\begin{equation}
 \rho:=\kappa_N^{1/N},\qquad
 M:=\max_{0\le r<N}\left(\frac{\bar A}{\rho}\right)^r.
 \label{eq:widen-Mrho}
\end{equation}
Then, for every $k\ge j$ and every admitted switching sequence,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\|\widetilde\Psi(k,j)\|\le M\rho^{k-j}.}
 \label{eq:widen-constructive-ues}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $k-j=qN+r$ with $0\le r<N$.  Submultiplicativity gives
\begin{equation}
 \|\widetilde\Psi(k,j)\|
 \le \bar A^r\kappa_N^q
 =\left(\frac{\bar A}{\rho}\right)^r\rho^{k-j}
 \le M\rho^{k-j}.
\end{equation}
\end{proof}

Choose $\mat Q=\mat I$ in the converse Lyapunov series and define
\begin{equation}
 \mat P_k:=\sum_{j=k}^{\infty}
 \widetilde\Psi(j,k)^\top\widetilde\Psi(j,k).
 \label{eq:widen-P-identity}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit converse-Lyapunov bounds]
\label{thm:widen-explicit-P}
Under Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-constructive-ues}, the Lyapunov sequence in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-P-identity} satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mat I\preceq\mat P_k\preceq
 p_+^{\rm an}\mat I,
 \qquad
 p_+^{\rm an}:=\frac{M^2}{1-\rho^2},}
 \label{eq:widen-P-bounds}
\end{equation}
and the decrease identity is exact:
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \widetilde\mat A_k^{{\rm cl}\top}\mat P_{k+1}
 \widetilde\mat A_k^{\rm cl}-\mat P_k=-\mat I.}
 \label{eq:widen-P-decrease}
\end{equation}
Thus the constants in Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-converse-decrease} may be chosen as
\begin{equation}
 p_-^{\rm an}=1,\qquad
 p_+^{\rm an}=\frac{M^2}{1-\rho^2},\qquad
 q_-^{\rm an}=1.
 \label{eq:widen-pq-constants}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The $j=k$ term in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-P-identity} is $\mat I$, giving the lower
bound.  Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-constructive-ues} gives
\begin{equation}
 \mat P_k\preceq
 \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}M^2\rho^{2n}\mat I
 =\frac{M^2}{1-\rho^2}\mat I.
\end{equation}
Shifting the infinite series by one step gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-P-decrease} exactly.
\end{proof}

Substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-pq-constants} into the nonlinear condition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-basin} produces a directly usable upper bound on the
structured nonlinear load.  In particular, it is sufficient that
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \ell_{\mathcal T}<\ell_*:=
 \sqrt{\bar A^2+\frac{1}{2p_+^{\rm an}}}-\bar A.}
 \label{eq:widen-ell-star}
\end{equation}
Indeed, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-ell-star} is exactly the positive root of
$p_+^{\rm an}\ell^2+2p_+^{\rm an}\bar A\ell-1/2<0$.

\begin{remark}[Finite rather than trajectory-specific certification]
\label{rem:widen-finite-certificate}
Phase B does not require an infinite-horizon simulation to identify
$p_\pm$ or $q_-$.  The remaining contraction quantity $\kappa_N$ is a
finite-window supremum over the declared compact matrix family.  It may be
bounded symbolically, by interval arithmetic, or by another rigorous compact
optimizer without changing the theorem.  Once a strict $\kappa_N<1$ is
available, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-Mrho}--\eqref{eq:widen-ell-star} turn it into the
Lyapunov and nonlinear margins analytically.
\end{remark}